# example/git/commit/gen-git-commit-message.wal
# Demo: Generate Conventional Commits message from staged diff.
#
# Two approaches compared below.
#
# === Approach 1: variable substitution (commented out) ===
#
#   - (context) ! python scripts/utils/commit_context.py
#   - (gen) ${context} Write a single Conventional Commits commit message...
#
# The shell output is captured into a variable ${context}, then inlined
# into the prompt. The agent receives a single assembled message — all
# context is packed into one request. This is like building a message,
# not being the message list. Each step is self-contained, sharing
# nothing across step boundaries.
#
# === Approach 2: memory accumulation (active) ===
#
#   - ! cat prompt/git/commit/conventional-commits.md
#   - ! git --no-pager diff --staged
#   - Write a single Conventional Commits commit message...
#
# Each - ! step appends its result to agent memory. The plain step
# inherits all previous context without variable plumbing. The WAL
# script itself is the message list — steps map 1:1 to entries in
# the conversation history. Simpler and more faithful to the
# WAL-as-message-list concept.

- ! cat prompt/git/commit/conventional-commits.md
- ! git --no-pager diff --staged
- Write a single Conventional Commits commit message for this diff, no explanation.
